\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s55 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Protocol Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The NSMenuItem protocol declares methods that are used to manipulate command items in menus. With some implementations of the OpenStep specification (including NeXT\'27s OPENSTEP), you cannot replace the NSMenuItem class with a different class which conforms to the NSMenuItem protocol. You may, however, subclass the NSMenuItem class if necessary.\
\fs14\
\fs28 See the NSMenu and NSMenuItem class specifications for more information on menus.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Keyboard Equivalents\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 An object conforming to the NSMenuItem protocol can be assigned a keyboard eqivalent, so that when the user types it the menu item\'27s action is sent. The keyboard eqivalent is defined in two parts. First is the basic key equivalent, which must be a Unicode character that can be generated by a single key press without modifier keys (Shift excepted). It is also possible to use a sequence of Unicode characters so long as the user\'27s key mapping is able to generate the sequence with a single key press. The basic key equivalent is set using \b setKeyEquivalent:\b0  and returned by \b keyEquivalent\b0 . The second part defines the modifier keys that must also be pressed. This is set using \b setKeyEquivalentModifierMask:\b0  and returned by \b keyEquivalentModifierMask\b0 . The modifier mask by default includes NSCommandKeyMask, and may also include the masks for the Shift, Alternate, or other modifier keys. Specifying keyboard equivalents in two parts allows you to define a modified keyboard equivalent without having to know which character is generated by the basic key plus the modifier. For example, you can define the keyboard equivalent Command-Alt-f without having to know which character is generated by typing Alt-f.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Certain methods in the NSMenuItem protocol can override assigned keyboard equivalents with those the user has specified in the defaults system. The \b setUsesUserKeyEquivalents:\b0  protocol method turns this behavior on or off, and \b usesUserKeyEquivalents\b0  returns its status. To determine the user-defined key equivalent for an NSMenuItem object, invoke the \b userKeyEquivalent\b0  instance method. If user-defined key equivalents are active and an NSMenuItem object has a user-defined key equivalent, its \b keyEquivalent\b0  method returns the user-defined key equivalent and not the one set using \b setKeyEquivalent:\b0 .\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Mnemonics\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 On certain platforms, currently including Microsoft Windows, an object conforming to the NSMenuItem protocol can also be assigned a mnemonic. Mnemonics can be assigned on other platforms as well, however, they won\'27t have any effect. Mnemonics are represented by an underlined character in the title of a menu item. The mnemonic can be any character that can be generated by a single key press without modifier keys (Shift excepted). When the menu is active, the user can type the underlined character in the menu item in order to activate that menu item. On Microsoft Windows a user activates the menu by pressing the Alternate key. A particular mnemonic character should only be used once within the set of menu items contained either in the same menu as the menu item or in the application\'27s main menu. \
\fs14\
